.TH VIF 1 2026-11-11 "vif" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vif \- a GPU terminal with command blocks and shells that outlive the window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vif
[\fB\-e\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...]]
.br
.B vif
\fIsubcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vif
is a terminal emulator that draws on the GPU. Every command is a block with its
exit code and time; shells keep running in a session host when their window
closes and come back in the next vif; hints pick links, paths and commit hashes
off the screen; a search covers every pane and the commands kept on this
machine.
.PP
With no arguments vif opens a window, taking back the sessions no window has.
With
.B \-e
it runs \fIcommand\fR in the window instead of the shell.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Show problems in vif.toml, with the line and what was probably meant.
.TP
.BI set " key value"
Change one setting, keeping the file's comments: \fBvif set window.opacity
0.9\fR.
.TP
.BR history " [\fIwords\fR...]"
Commands kept with their output, found by what they were or printed.
.TP
.B sessions
Every shell vif keeps running, and which have no window.
.TP
.BI attach " name" | id
Open a window for one of them.
.TP
.B quick
Show or hide the quick terminal; bind it to a key in the compositor.
.TP
.B niri
Print a fragment for niri's config: the quick terminal's place and key.
.TP
.BR settings " [\fIpage\fR]"
Open the settings window, on a page like \fBfont\fR or \fBkeys\fR.
.TP
.BR theme " [\fBuse\fR \fIname\fR | \fBnew\fR \fIname\fR [\fIfrom\fR] | \fBadd\fR \fIfile\fR|\fIurl\fR | \fBcheck\fR [\fIname\fR]]"
List themes, switch every window to one, start your own, install one (vif's
own, or colors from kitty, Ghostty, Alacritty, iTerm, base16, Xresources,
Windows Terminal or pywal), or show a theme's problems.
.TP
.BR ssh " [\fB\-\-udp\fR|\fB\-\-no\-udp\fR] \fIhost\fR [\fIname\fR]"
A shell on \fIhost\fR that outlives the connection; vif has to be installed
there too. Where UDP gets through, keys and output travel as encrypted
datagrams on a port from 60000 to 61000, the way mosh does, so the session
survives sleep and network changes; elsewhere it stays on ssh and reconnects.
\fIname\fR comes back to the same shell later. ctrl-^ \fB.\fR leaves it
running.
.TP
.BI gif " file" " [\-\-blocks] [\-\-mono] [\-\-width " n "] [\-\-once] [\-\-text]"
Play a GIF in text where you are.
.TP
.BR greet " [\fIfile\fR]"
Show the greeting from \fB[greeting]\fR, or \fIfile\fR in its picture's place.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/vif/vif.toml
The configuration; open windows follow it as it is saved.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vif/themes/
Themes of your own.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/vif/archive/
Commands kept with what they printed, readable by you alone, secrets taken out.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vif\-sessions/
The session hosts' sockets.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VIF_CONFIG
Read this file instead of vif.toml.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the files above go. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is used only when it is your own
folder that nobody else can write into; otherwise vif makes a closed one in the
temporary folder.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR mosh (1),
.BR tmux (1)
.PP
https://github.com/Pisosenie/vif
